Eastman Kodak Company v. Suzhou Koda Hydraulic Elevator Co., Ltd. — trademark infringement dispute

【Ruling Summary】

1. In adjudicating cases involving trademark infringement disputes, the People's Court's determination of whether the trademark in question is a well-known trademark constitutes a determination of a basic fact of the case and is not restricted by the claims of the parties.

2. Pursuant to Article 1, Item (2) of the Supreme People's Court's Interpretation on Several Issues Concerning the Application of Law in the Trial of Civil Disputes over Trademarks, the act of reproducing, imitating, or translating another person's registered well-known trademark or its essential part and using it as a trademark on goods that are not identical or similar to those for which the well-known trademark is registered, thereby misleading the public and potentially damaging the interests of the registrant of the well-known trademark, constitutes an act of causing other damage to the exclusive right to use a registered trademark as specified in Article 52, Item (5) of the Trademark Law, and the corresponding civil liability shall be borne in accordance with the law.

Plaintiff: Eastman Kodak Company, domiciled at State Road, Rochester, New York, USA.

Legal Representative: Joyce P. Haag, Secretary of the Company.

Defendant: Suzhou Koda Hydraulic Elevator Co., Ltd., domiciled at East of Lixing Station, Beishe Town, Wujiang City, Jiangsu Province.

Legal Representative: Ren Fuming, Chairman of the Company.

Eastman Kodak Company (hereinafter referred to as Eastman) filed a lawsuit with the Suzhou Intermediate People's Court, Jiangsu Province, against Suzhou Koda Hydraulic Elevator Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as Koda Elevator Company) for trademark infringement.

The plaintiff Eastman alleged: Eastman was founded in 1880 and is a well-known multinational corporation producing traditional and digital imaging products, medical imaging products, commercial photography products, optical components, and displays. As early as 1888, Eastman used "KODAK" as a trademark on cameras, with a history of 117 years. As a world-renowned enterprise, Eastman has registered nearly 1,700 "KODAK" trademarks or trademarks with "KODAK" as the main text in more than 150 countries and regions, possessing extremely high visibility and a good market reputation. In China, Eastman's "KODAK" trademark was approved for registration in Class 1 "Organic and inorganic chemicals" as early as 1982, with registration number 154121. On this basis, Eastman has registered multiple "KODAK" trademarks in Class 1 and Class 9 goods. As the world's largest producer and supplier of imaging products and related services, Eastman's "KODAK" trademark has long become a well-known trademark both at home and abroad. The State Administration for Industry and Commerce of China has twice included the "KODAK Kodak" trademark in the "National Key Trademark Protection List" in 1999 and 2000, and many Chinese courts, industry and commerce enforcement agencies, and customs have also recognized and investigated numerous infringements of Eastman's "KODAK" trademark.

In June 2005, the plaintiff Eastman discovered that the escalators used at Chengwaicheng Home Culture Plaza and China First Shopping Center bore the "KODAK" mark. Investigation revealed that the escalators were produced and sold by the defendant Koda Elevator Company. In addition to elevator products, Koda Elevator Company and its Beijing Branch and Guangzhou Branch also used the "KODAK" mark on their corporate website, factory gate, company nameplate, employee business cards, product introductions, and corporate promotional materials. Meanwhile, Koda Elevator Company and its Beijing Branch also registered the "KODAK" trademark as their website domain names kodaklift.com.cn and kodak-bj.com and used them. Without authorization, Koda Elevator Company used a trademark identical to Eastman's registered "KODAK" trademark on its products, promotional materials, and company website, and registered "KODAK" as its domain name, infringing Eastman's exclusive right to use its registered trademark and damaging the image of Eastman's "KODAK" trademark. Eastman requested the court to: 1. order Koda Elevator Company to immediately cease all infringements of the "KODAK" trademark; 2. order Koda Elevator Company to compensate Eastman for economic losses of RMB 500,000 (including reasonable expenses incurred by Eastman) for its infringement; 3. order Koda Elevator Company to publish a notice in national newspapers to publicly eliminate the adverse effects of its infringement.

The plaintiff Eastman submitted the following evidence:

The first set of evidence was ownership evidence, including:

1. 50 trademark registration certificates for the "KODAK" trademark in 13 countries or regions worldwide;
2. Trademark registration certificates for the "KODAK" trademark in Class 1 and Class 9 goods in China;
3. Advertising and promotional materials for products bearing the "KODAK" trademark in China, including special reports in media such as magazine advertisements and television commercials;
4. Excerpts from the State Administration for Industry and Commerce Trademark Office's 1999 and 2000 "Key Trademark Protection List";
5. The 2005 "World's Top 500 Brands" ranking by the World Brand Lab;
6. The 2005 "World Famous Brand 500" jointly selected by organizations including the Patriotic Chinese Chamber of Commerce World Famous Enterprise Alliance, the U.S.-China Economic and Trade Investment Promotion Council, the World Brand Organization, and the Global Chinese Famous Brand Network;
7. Notarized materials (Beijing Guoxin Notary Office [2005] Jing Guo Zheng Min Zi No. 12693) from the National Bureau of Statistics website showing "2000, 2001, 2002 World's Top 500 Largest Enterprises by Turnover";
8. Notarized materials (Beijing Guoxin Notary Office [2005] Jing Guo Zheng Min Zi No. 12694) from the Ministry of Commerce website showing "2002, 2003 Top 500 Foreign-Invested Enterprises by Sales", "2003 Top 100 Foreign-Invested Enterprises by Export", "2002-2003 Largest 500 Foreign-Invested Enterprises in China", and "January-December 2004 Top 100 Foreign-Invested Enterprises by Export";
9. Internet news reports on Eastman's sponsorship of the 2008 Beijing Olympics;
10. Excerpts and translations of the world's top 100 brands from the Interbrand column of BusinessWeek magazine.

The above evidence was used to prove that Eastman is the owner of the "KODAK" trademark and that through extensive registration and use, its "KODAK" trademark has gained high visibility in China and the world.

The second set of evidence was infringement evidence, including:

11. Notarized evidence preservation (Beijing Guoxin Notary Office [2005] Jing Guo Zheng Min Zi No. 06304) of the elevators used at China First Shopping Center and Chengwaicheng Home Culture Plaza, applied for by Eastman;
12. Notarized evidence preservation (Beijing Guoxin Notary Office [2005] Jing Guo Zheng Min Zi No. 06306) of the act of obtaining relevant materials from the defendant Koda Elevator Company's Beijing Branch, applied for by Eastman;
13. Notarized evidence preservation (Beijing Guoxin Notary Office [2005] Jing Guo Zheng Min Zi No. 06307) of Koda Elevator Company's domain name registration information and website content, proving that Koda Elevator Company registered Eastman's "KODAK" trademark as a domain name;
14. Notarized evidence preservation (Beijing Guoxin Notary Office [2005] Jing Guo Zheng Min Zi No. 12692) of the domain name registration information and website content of Koda Elevator Company's Beijing Branch;
15. Notarized evidence preservation (Beijing Guoxin Notary Office [2005] Jing Guo Zheng Min Zi No. 12691) of the website content of Koda Elevator Company's Guangzhou Branch.

The above evidence was used to prove that Koda Elevator Company, without permission, used the "KODAK" mark on the elevator products it produced and sold, as well as on product catalogs and corporate materials, and registered Eastman's "KODAK" trademark as its website domain name, thereby infringing Eastman's trademark exclusive right.

The third set of evidence was supplementary evidence, including:

16. Invoices for translation fees, travel expenses, and notarization fees incurred by Eastman for this case, totaling RMB 6,687, to prove the amount of reasonable expenses incurred by Eastman for this case;
17. Copies of business licenses of Eastman's main investment companies in China, to prove that Eastman entered the Chinese market early, has high visibility in China, and has a wide scope of use;
18. Trademark registration certificates for Eastman's No. 154121, 1120489, 926799, 931258, used together with evidence 2;
19. Filing notices for trademark license contracts for Eastman's No. 1120489, 314885, 972070, 1134076, 940003, 271252;
20. Trademark registration certificates for Eastman's No. 314885, 383891, 731756, 994084, 2017616, 775878, 1353765, 1322594, 735359, 1332395;
21. News reports on Eastman's "KODAK" brand sponsorship of the 2008 Beijing Olympics, used together with evidence 9;
22. Invoices for advertising and promotional fees for "KODAK" issued by Shanghai Ogilvy & Mather Advertising Co., Ltd.;
23. Certificates issued by Kodak (China) Co., Ltd. (an associated company of Eastman) regarding sales of products bearing the "KODAK" registered trademark sold by Eastman's main investment companies in China from 2002 to 2005, taxes paid to the state, and advertising investment data, as well as the budget amount for Eastman's sponsorship of the Beijing Olympics;
24. A report from Sina.com titled "BusinessWeek Ranks Top 100 Global Brands, No Chinese Company on List";
25. The "2005 Fortune 500 List" reported by CNN (Cable News Network) Finance Channel and the "2004 Fortune 500 List" published on the USATODAY website;
26. The "2005 Chinese Consumers' Favorite and Satisfied Brands" published on the China Quality Trustworthy Product Procurement Guide website;
27. A report reprinted by Xinhuanet from the Los Angeles Times titled "Kodak Surpasses Sony for First Time in U.S. Digital Camera Market";
28. An article about Eastman published on the Guangdong Information Network.

The above evidence was also used to prove that Eastman is the owner of the "KODAK" trademark and that through extensive advertising, its "KODAK" trademark has high visibility.

29. The case of Eastman Kodak Co. v. DBRAKOW (1898), excerpts from the 1995 U.S. Congressional Record, the case of Eastman Photographic Materials Co. v. John Griffiths Cycle Co. (1898), and excerpts from McCarthy on Trademarks and Unfair Competition. Used to prove that the cross-class protection cases of Eastman's registered "KODAK" trademark have become classic cases in U.S. and U.K. judicial precedents, and the exclusivity of its trademark has been confirmed by various countries.

30. Translation fee invoices totaling RMB 3,304. Used together with evidence 1 to prove Eastman's reasonable expenses for this case.

31. Notarized documents and translations of Eastman's "KODAK" trademark registration certificates in three countries and regions including China, the United States, Canada, and Hong Kong;
32. Translation fee invoices totaling RMB 17,317. Used together with evidence 1 to prove Eastman's reasonable expenses for this case.
33. The "Digital Camera Shooting Guide" magazine, which records that the world's first camera was produced in 1888 bearing the "KODAK" trademark, proving the long history of Kodak cameras.

The defendant Koda Elevator Company argued: 1. Koda Elevator Company operates products that are not in the same or similar class as those of the plaintiff Eastman. To find Koda Elevator Company liable for trademark infringement, the primary prerequisite is to determine that the "KODAK" trademark is a well-known trademark, and only then can cross-class protection be applied. Whether Eastman's registered "KODAK" trademark is a well-known trademark should be determined by the court in accordance with the law; 2. Even if Eastman's registered "KODAK" trademark is recognized as a well-known trademark, Koda Elevator Company's conduct does not constitute infringement because: (1) Koda Elevator Company's use of the word "KODAK" is merely an English translation of its trade name "Koda" (科达) and is not used as a product trademark. Its elevator products are neither identical nor similar to the goods for which Eastman's registered "KODAK" trademark is approved for use, and would not cause public confusion or misidentification. Koda Elevator Company's use of the word "KODAK" does not cause substantial damage to Eastman's interests; (2) The domain names kodaklift.com.cn and kodak-bj.com registered by Koda Elevator Company are used for promoting elevator products, not for conducting transactions of goods identical or similar to Eastman's, and there is no effect or consequence of causing relevant public misidentification; 3. Eastman has no evidence that Koda Elevator Company's use of the word "KODAK" caused a reduction in its product sales or other economic losses. Eastman's claim for RMB 500,000 in economic damages is unfounded; 4. Koda Elevator Company used the "KODAK" mark on a very small number of elevator products and obtained very little profit. In summary, Koda Elevator Company requested the court to reject Eastman's claims.

The defendant Koda Elevator Company did not provide evidence.

The Suzhou Intermediate People's Court organized the examination of evidence in accordance with the law.

The defendant Koda Elevator Company did not object to the authenticity of the notarized evidentiary materials submitted by the plaintiff Eastman, nor did it object to the objectivity of the online information materials involved in the evidence submitted by Eastman, but it could not confirm the authenticity of the content of the said online information materials and believed that some of the evidentiary materials submitted by Eastman concerned the promotion of Eastman itself, not the promotion of the "KODAK" registered trademark, and were irrelevant to this case. With respect to the relevant cases submitted by Eastman, Koda Elevator Company argued that case law is not applicable in China and that U.S. judicial cases and their judicial theories cannot be used as reference. Moreover, the theory of trademark dilution involved in the U.S. cases submitted by Eastman is highly controversial even in the United States.

The Suzhou Intermediate People's Court found at first instance:

The plaintiff Eastman was founded in 1880. In 1888, Eastman created the "KODAK" brand and used it on camera products; in 1889, Eastman produced rolls of film using nitrocellulose base; in 1891, it produced film that could be loaded and unloaded by photographers themselves. The emergence and popularization of "KODAK" cameras and film promoted the popularization of photography, leading to the maturity and rapid development of cameras and film globally. Eastman gradually developed into the world's largest producer and supplier of imaging products and related services, and its "KODAK" brand became a world-famous brand. Over the course of a century of development, Eastman has continuously promoted and used the "KODAK" trademark globally, spending substantial sums on its promotion, including being an Olympic brand sponsor for forty consecutive years. To date, Eastman has widely registered the "KODAK" trademark or trademarks with "KODAK" as the main text in various countries and regions around the world, and the "KODAK" trademark enjoys extremely high visibility and a good market reputation.

In China, the plaintiff Eastman filed an application for registration of the "Kodak" trademark with the Trademark Office on September 27, 1979. In February 1982, Eastman obtained registration of the "KODAK" English trademark in Class 1 "Organic and inorganic chemicals" under registration number 154121. On this basis, Eastman registered multiple "KODAK", "Kodak", "Kodak" word marks and "K"-shaped pattern trademarks in Class 1 and Class 9 goods, and applied for registration of the corresponding Chinese trademark "柯达". Eastman has conducted extensive and high-intensity advertising campaigns for its "KODAK" and "柯达" trademarks through various media in China for several decades, establishing Kodak film developing and printing franchise stores throughout the Chinese market, achieving a high market share. Its "KODAK" trademark is widely known by the Chinese public. The State Administration for Industry and Commerce of China twice included the "KODAK Kodak" trademark in the "National Key Trademark Protection List" in 1999 and 2000.

The defendant Koda Elevator Company was established in 2001, with its business scope being "production, sale of various types of elevators, escalators, and elevator installation, maintenance, repair, and after-sales service". Koda Elevator Company uses a stylized "科达" word and graphic as a registered trademark brand for producing and selling its elevator products. Since early 2005, Koda Elevator Company has prominently displayed the word "KODAK" on its elevator products, company nameplates, employee business cards, product introductions, and corporate promotional materials, either as an independent mark or in a vertical arrangement with its "科达" mark. Koda Elevator Company and its Beijing Branch also applied to the China Internet Network Information Center for registration of the domain names kodaklift.com.cn and kodak-bj.com for online promotion and business. After discovering this, the plaintiff Eastman filed this lawsuit.

It was also found that the plaintiff Eastman had actually paid notarization fees, translation fees, and other expenses totaling RMB 27,308 in the process of collecting evidence for this lawsuit.

The issues in dispute in this case were: 1. Whether the "KODAK" trademark owned by the plaintiff Eastman should be protected as a well-known trademark; 2. Whether the defendant Koda Elevator Company's use of the "KODAK" business mark constitutes infringement of Eastman's registered "KODAK" trademark rights, and if so, how Koda Elevator Company should bear liability.

The Suzhou Intermediate People's Court held at first instance:

In this case, the plaintiff Eastman alleged that the defendant Koda Elevator Company infringed its civil rights, constituting infringement of its exclusive right to use a registered trademark, and accordingly requested the court to investigate Koda Elevator Company's civil infringement liability. Since Eastman is incorporated in the United States and is a U.S. legal person, and both China and the United States are members of the Paris Convention for the Protection of Industrial Property and the World Trade Organization, this case shall be governed by relevant Chinese laws, the Paris Convention, and the TRIPS Agreement.

First, the "KODAK" registered trademark owned by the plaintiff Eastman should be recognized as a well-known trademark and accorded corresponding protection in accordance with the law.

According to the provisions of Article 14 of the Trademark Law of the People's Republic of China (hereinafter referred to as the Trademark Law), the following factors shall be considered in determining a well-known trademark: (1) the degree of knowledge of the trademark among the relevant public; (2) the duration of use of the trademark; (3) the duration, extent, and geographical scope of any advertising or promotion of the trademark; (4) the records of protection of the trademark as a well-known trademark; and (5) other factors contributing to the fame of the trademark.

Based on the facts of this case, the registered trademark "KODAK" is a fanciful commercial mark created by the plaintiff, Eastman Company, in 1888, and has been widely registered globally. Through over 100 years of extensive and continuous advertising and promotion by Eastman Company, as well as the good quality of goods bearing the "KODAK" trademark, Eastman Company's "KODAK" products have a broad user base worldwide, are well known to the public, and have long enjoyed a high reputation.

China is also a major market for the plaintiff, Eastman Company. The "KODAK" trademark was first registered in China as early as 1979, and Eastman Company enjoys exclusive rights to the trademark. Eastman Company has invested substantial sums over many years in continuous and extensive advertising and promotion of the trademark and its goods. Traditional and digital imaging products under the "KODAK" brand have a large consumer base in China. At the same time, because "KODAK" traditional and digital imaging products are in fact closely linked to the daily lives of the general public, the "KODAK" trademark has actually become a household commercial brand in China.

In view of the above facts, it can be determined that the registered trademark "KODAK" owned by the plaintiff, Eastman Company, is a trademark that enjoys a high reputation in the market and is well known to the relevant public. In judicial protection, this registered trademark should be recognized as a well-known trademark and, in accordance with the law, afforded a high level of protection across different goods or services.

The defendant, Keda Elevator Company, argued in its defense that the plaintiff, Eastman Company, did not independently seek a finding that the "KODAK" registered trademark is a well-known trademark in its claims, and that the "KODAK" registered trademark has not been recognized as a well-known trademark by China's trademark administrative authorities. Therefore, it argued that the trademark cannot be recognized as a well-known trademark and afforded corresponding legal protection. On this point, the court held that in adjudicating cases involving trademark infringement disputes, a people's court's determination of whether the trademark in question constitutes a well-known trademark is a determination of a basic fact of the case and is not restricted by the parties' claims. Based on the factual findings, the trademark in question that constitutes a well-known trademark shall be afforded corresponding legal protection in accordance with the law. Therefore, the above defense arguments of Keda Elevator Company cannot stand and are not supported.

Second, the defendant, Keda Elevator Company's use of the "KODAK" commercial mark constitutes infringement of the "KODAK" registered trademark rights owned by the plaintiff, Eastman Company.

The defendant, Keda Elevator Company, printed the independent and prominent textual mark "KODAK" on its goods, company doorplate, employee business cards, and corporate promotional materials. Keda Elevator Company and its branches also applied to register the domain names kodaklift.com.cn and kodak-bj.com using "KODAK" as the commercial mark index, and used these domain names for online promotion and business operations. In this lawsuit, although Keda Elevator Company explained that the "KODAK" commercial mark it used was the English translation of its corporate name "科达" (Keda) and was not used as a product trademark, based on the actual form of use, Keda Elevator Company was clearly using the "KODAK" commercial mark prominently in the form of a product trademark. Furthermore, as previously stated, "KODAK" is a fanciful commercial mark created by the plaintiff, Eastman Company, and has no other literal meaning. The mark directly refers to Eastman Company and is a distinctive and prominent commercial mark of Eastman Company. Although Keda Elevator Company claimed that the "KODAK" commercial mark it used was an English translation of its corporate name "科达," there is no linguistic correspondence in Chinese-English translation between "KODAK" and "科达," nor does it conform to the English translation conventions or phonetic habits of the Chinese language. On this point, Keda Elevator Company also failed to provide further reasonable explanation. Considering the facts that the "KODAK" registered trademark owned by Eastman Company and the "柯达" (Keda) trademark have long been widely known to the public, it can be determined that Keda Elevator Company's use of "KODAK" to represent its corporate name "科达" is clearly a reproduction or imitation of the corresponding relationship between Eastman Company's well-known trademarks "柯达" and "KODAK." Therefore, Keda Elevator Company's defense argument that its "KODAK" mark was a linguistic translation of its corporate name "科达" and that its use of the mark constituted fair use cannot stand.

According to Article 1, paragraph (2) of the Supreme People's Court's Interpretation on Several Issues Concerning the Application of Law in the Trial of Civil Disputes over Trademarks, the act of reproducing, imitating, or translating another person's registered well-known trademark or its essential part and using it as a trademark on goods that are not identical or similar to those for which the trademark is registered, thereby misleading the public and potentially causing damage to the interests of the owner of the registered well-known trademark, constitutes an act of causing other damage to another person's exclusive right to use a registered trademark as stipulated in Article 52, paragraph (5) of the Trademark Law. Corresponding to this provision, Article 6bis of the Paris Convention for the Protection of Industrial Property provides: "The countries of the Union undertake, ex officio if their legislation so permits, or at the request of an interested party, to refuse or to cancel the registration, and to prohibit the use, of a trademark which constitutes a reproduction, an imitation, or a translation, liable to create confusion, of a mark considered by the competent authority of the country of registration or use to be well known in that country as being already the mark of a person entitled to the benefits of this Convention and used for identical or similar goods. These provisions shall also apply when the essential part of the mark constitutes a reproduction of any such well-known mark or an imitation liable to create confusion therewith." The TRIPS Agreement, in its Part II—"Standards concerning the availability, scope and use of Intellectual Property Rights"—Section 2: "Trademarks", Article 16: "Rights Conferred", paragraph 2, further provides: "Article 6bis of the Paris Convention (1967) shall apply, mutatis mutandis, to goods or services which are not similar to those for which a trademark is registered, provided that use of that trademark in relation to those goods or services would indicate a connection between those goods or services and the owner of the registered trademark and provided that the interests of the owner of the registered trademark are likely to be damaged by such use." Combining the facts of this case and the above legal norms, the defendant, Keda Elevator Company's use of the "KODAK" mark is clearly an imitation of the "KODAK" well-known trademark owned by the plaintiff, Eastman Company, and is intended to free-ride on the good reputation of that trademark to obtain improper commercial benefits. From the perspective of protecting the exclusivity of a well-known trademark, Keda Elevator Company's use of the "KODAK" mark will inevitably reduce the distinctiveness of the "KODAK" well-known trademark owned by Eastman Company, damage its goodwill value, and cause material harm to the exclusivity and image interests of the "KODAK" well-known trademark owned by Eastman Company. Therefore, Keda Elevator Company's act of using the "KODAK" commercial mark without the consent of the owner of the "KODAK" well-known trademark constitutes trademark infringement. Keda Elevator Company's defense argument that its use of the "KODAK" wording does not cause material harm to Eastman Company's interests and therefore does not constitute infringement cannot stand and is not supported by law.

The defendant, Keda Elevator Company, by reproducing and imitating the "KODAK" well-known trademark, infringed upon the exclusive right to use the "KODAK" trademark owned by the plaintiff, Eastman Company, and shall bear civil liability for infringement, including ceasing the infringement, eliminating the adverse effects, and compensating for economic losses. Eastman Company's claim that Keda Elevator Company immediately cease all infringing acts against the "KODAK" trademark and publish notices in national newspapers to publicly eliminate the adverse effects shall be supported in accordance with the law. Regarding Eastman Company's claim for an order that Keda Elevator Company pay economic damages of RMB 500,000 (including Eastman Company's reasonable expenses), given that Keda Elevator Company does not engage in the production or sale of goods identical to Eastman Company's, it is inappropriate to base the calculation primarily on the direct operating profits or losses of each party's products. Therefore, in determining the amount of economic compensation in this case, the court shall, with reference to factors such as the benefits Keda Elevator Company may have obtained from its use of the "KODAK" trademark and the reasonable expenses incurred by Eastman Company in stopping the infringement in question, determine at its discretion the economic compensation liability that Keda Elevator Company shall bear. The portion of Eastman Company's claim for excessive damages is not supported.

Accordingly, the Suzhou Intermediate People's Court, in accordance with Articles 14, 52, and 56 of the Trademark Law, Article 11 of the Supreme People's Court's Several Provisions on Evidence in Civil Proceedings, and Article 1, paragraph (2) and Article 22 of the Supreme People's Court's Interpretation on Several Issues Concerning the Application of Law in the Trial of Civil Disputes over Trademarks, hereby enters the following judgment:

1. The defendant, Keda Elevator Company, shall immediately cease using the "KODAK" commercial mark;

2. The defendant, Keda Elevator Company, shall pay the plaintiff, Eastman Company, economic damages of RMB 50,000, to be fulfilled within 10 days after this judgment takes effect;

3. The defendant, Keda Elevator Company, shall, within 15 days from the date this judgment takes effect, publish a notice once in each of two national newspapers regarding the trademark infringement facts and trademark prohibition matters involved in this case, to eliminate the adverse effects. The content of the published notice must be reviewed by the court. If the notice is not published within the time limit, the court will publish the content of this judgment publicly, and the relevant expenses shall be borne by the defendant, Keda Elevator Company.

4. The remaining claims of the plaintiff, Eastman Company, are rejected.

After the judgment of first instance was pronounced, neither party appealed, and the judgment of first instance has taken legal effect.
